1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:04,121
Nine fifty nine in the morning. The south tower has just come down.

2
00:00:04,121 --> 00:00:08,420
It stood fifty six minutes after it was hit, then it was gone in ten seconds.

3
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:14,272
Inside the north tower, two hundred metres away, there are still firefighters climbing.

4
00:00:14,272 --> 00:00:19,385
Within about a minute, an order goes out over the radio from the lobby command post. Evacuate.

5
00:00:19,385 --> 00:00:20,690
Get out of the building.

6
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:25,335
This film is about that order, and about a dozen others like it.

7
00:00:25,335 --> 00:00:30,413
Because the thing that keeps happening on September eleventh, over and over, in every agency,

8
00:00:30,413 --> 00:00:35,328
in every building, is that somebody says the right thing at the right time and it does not

9
00:00:35,328 --> 00:00:35,710
arrive.

10
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,558
At four minutes past ten, a New York police helicopter hovering beside the north tower radios

11
00:00:41,558 --> 00:00:45,820
that the top fifteen storeys are glowing red and the building is going to come down.

12
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:51,721
Four minutes later the same pilot says he does not believe the tower will stand much longer.

13
00:00:51,721 --> 00:00:54,400
Both transmissions are real. Both are on the record.

14
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,780
And the fire department commanders in the lobby of that tower do not hear either one.

15
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:01,318
Not because they ignored it.

16
00:01:01,318 --> 00:01:05,894
Because they had no radio in that lobby capable of receiving a police department frequency.

17
00:01:05,894 --> 00:01:10,671
The two departments standing in the same disaster were not on the same system, and there was no

18
00:01:10,671 --> 00:01:12,180
way for one to hear the other.

19
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:19,040
At ten twenty eight, the north tower comes down. It had stood for a hundred and two minutes.

20
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:25,325
Twenty three minutes before that, in Washington, the Vice President had authorised fighter

21
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:27,669
pilots to shoot down a hijacked airliner.

22
00:01:27,669 --> 00:01:32,813
The nine eleven Commission established, years later, that the pilots were never given that

23
00:01:32,813 --> 00:01:33,156
order.

24
00:01:33,156 --> 00:01:38,416
The only instruction that ever reached them was to identify the type and tail number of what

25
00:01:38,416 --> 00:01:38,930
they saw.

26
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:45,587
This is the twenty fifth of September eleven, told through what was actually recorded.

27
00:01:45,587 --> 00:01:46,457
Not the footage.

28
00:01:46,457 --> 00:01:51,405
The tapes, the transcripts, the radio logs, and the reports that were written afterwards by

29
00:01:51,405 --> 00:01:54,070
people whose job was to find out what went wrong.

30
00:01:55,210 --> 00:01:57,160
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

31
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:01,780
September eleventh. The orders that never arrived.

32
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:07,480
The first warning anyone on the ground received that morning was sent by accident, to a man who

33
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,020
could not make out a word of it.

34
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:12,031
Seven fifty nine in the morning.

35
00:02:12,031 --> 00:02:17,113
American eleven, a seven six seven bound for Los Angeles, lifts off from Boston Logan.

36
00:02:17,113 --> 00:02:22,608
Fourteen minutes later a Boston Center controller named Pete Zalewski tells it to turn twenty

37
00:02:22,608 --> 00:02:24,735
degrees right. The aircraft answers.

38
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:28,340
It is the last instruction American eleven ever acknowledges.

39
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:31,968
At eight twenty one the transponder goes off.

40
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:35,452
That is the box that tells the ground who you are and how high.

41
00:02:35,452 --> 00:02:39,046
Without it the aircraft becomes an anonymous dot among thousands.

42
00:02:39,046 --> 00:02:43,580
The controller does not yet think hijacking. Nothing in his training points there.

43
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:48,286
But the ground is not actually the first place this gets reported.

44
00:02:48,286 --> 00:02:53,326
It is reported from inside the aircraft, by two flight attendants, on the telephone, while it

45
00:02:53,326 --> 00:02:54,030
is happening.

46
00:02:55,170 --> 00:03:00,207
At nineteen minutes past eight, Betty Ong reaches American Airlines reservations in Cary, North

47
00:03:00,207 --> 00:03:03,600
Carolina. She stays on that line for about twenty three minutes.

48
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:09,444
Two minutes later, Madeline Amy Sweeney reaches a flight services manager at American's own

49
00:03:09,444 --> 00:03:12,700
operations at Logan, and talks to him for about twelve minutes.

50
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,639
I am going to be careful with these two, because the record is careful with them.

51
00:03:17,639 --> 00:03:22,001
Several versions of what they said circulate, and they do not all match, and the Commission's

52
00:03:22,001 --> 00:03:24,300
own wording is not the wording most often quoted.

53
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:27,630
So I will tell you what they did rather than put words in their mouths.

54
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:33,220
They described what was happening in the cabin, to people on the ground, calmly enough that

55
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:34,830
those people could write it down.

56
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:39,367
For thirty five minutes, the only accurate account of the first hijacking in American history

57
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:42,880
to end in a building came from two members of cabin crew on a telephone.

58
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:46,966
And that is the pattern setting itself up.

59
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:51,673
The best information anyone had that morning was almost never in the hands of the people with

60
00:03:51,673 --> 00:03:53,040
the authority to act on it.

61
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:58,435
Because the procedure on the books assumed something very specific.

62
00:03:58,435 --> 00:04:00,366
It assumed the pilot would tell you.

63
00:04:00,366 --> 00:04:05,356
Over the radio, or by setting the transponder to seven five zero zero, the code for a hijack.

64
00:04:05,356 --> 00:04:10,400
The hijacking in the manual was a negotiation that would land somewhere and be talked through.

65
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:16,425
At twenty four minutes and thirty eight seconds past eight, a voice comes over the frequency.

66
00:04:16,425 --> 00:04:17,476
It is not the pilot.

67
00:04:17,476 --> 00:04:22,361
Whoever is holding the microphone in that cockpit has keyed the wrong one, and is speaking to

68
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:24,620
the cabin, and reaching the ground instead.

69
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,000
We have some planes.

70
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:30,103
Some planes. Plural.

71
00:04:30,103 --> 00:04:35,213
It is the single most important sentence spoken anywhere that morning, and here is what the

72
00:04:35,213 --> 00:04:38,526
record says about it. The controller could not make it out.

73
00:04:38,526 --> 00:04:40,940
What he heard was something unintelligible.

74
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:45,015
He replayed it. He asked colleagues to listen.

75
00:04:45,015 --> 00:04:49,939
It took roughly forty minutes before anyone in a position to act understood that the word was

76
00:04:49,939 --> 00:04:53,645
planes, and by then two of them had already been flown into buildings.

77
00:04:53,645 --> 00:04:57,880
The warning existed from twenty four minutes past eight. It just did not arrive.

78
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,910
Ten minutes after the first one, the same voice comes back.

79
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,870
Nobody move please. We are going back to the airport.

80
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,622
That one is clearer, and it tells the controller he has a hijacking.

81
00:05:12,622 --> 00:05:18,072
What it does not tell him, because the first transmission was unintelligible, is that the word

82
00:05:18,072 --> 00:05:18,710
was plural.

83
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:22,254
Think about what that one missing word costs.

84
00:05:22,254 --> 00:05:27,297
If some planes is understood at twenty four minutes past eight, then at that moment the entire

85
00:05:27,297 --> 00:05:32,233
system knows this is not one aeroplane and one negotiation. It knows to look for the others.

86
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:35,720
Nobody looks for the others until the second one has already hit.

87
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:41,127
At thirty four minutes past eight, Boston Center goes off the books.

88
00:05:41,127 --> 00:05:45,690
It tries to reach the military directly, through a base on Cape Cod, and through a site in

89
00:05:45,690 --> 00:05:48,275
Atlantic City that had already been decommissioned.

90
00:05:48,275 --> 00:05:52,280
There is no phone number written down anywhere for the thing that is happening.

91
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:58,320
There is a number that defines the whole morning, and it is not a big one. Nine minutes.

92
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:04,544
At thirty seven minutes and fifty two seconds past eight, Boston Center reaches the North East

93
00:06:04,544 --> 00:06:08,562
Air Defense Sector in Rome, New York, and says it has a hijacked aircraft.

94
00:06:08,562 --> 00:06:13,720
The Commission staff called that call the moment the air defence of America began that morning.

95
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:19,575
American eleven hit the north tower at forty six minutes and forty seconds past eight.

96
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:23,350
From the first military notification to impact is under nine minutes.

97
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:28,110
And nine minutes turned out to be the most warning anybody got about anything, all day.

98
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:33,110
Three minutes before that call, Boston Center had already gone off protocol.

99
00:06:33,110 --> 00:06:37,643
It tried to reach the military directly, through a base on Cape Cod and through a site in

100
00:06:37,643 --> 00:06:39,833
Atlantic City that had been decommissioned.

101
00:06:39,833 --> 00:06:44,620
There was no established line for what was happening, so people started improvising inside the

102
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:45,740
first fifteen minutes.

103
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:51,399
At forty six minutes past eight, fighters at Otis on Cape Cod are ordered to scramble.

104
00:06:51,399 --> 00:06:54,499
The order goes out before anyone has permission to give it.

105
00:06:54,499 --> 00:06:59,070
The commanders' own account is blunt. Go ahead and scramble, we'd get permission later.

106
00:07:00,210 --> 00:07:04,031
Those fighters get airborne from Otis at fifty three minutes past eight.

107
00:07:04,031 --> 00:07:08,647
And then they are held, in military airspace off Long Island, between eight minutes and

108
00:07:08,647 --> 00:07:10,080
thirteen minutes past nine.

109
00:07:11,210 --> 00:07:15,303
Not because anyone was being cautious. Because nobody could tell them where to go.

110
00:07:15,303 --> 00:07:16,950
There was no target to give them.

111
00:07:16,950 --> 00:07:21,692
The aircraft they had been scrambled for had already hit a building, and the second one had not

112
00:07:21,692 --> 00:07:23,090
been identified to them yet.

113
00:07:24,910 --> 00:07:29,900
At eight minutes past nine the sector makes a decision that, in hindsight, is the most sensible

114
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:34,891
thing anyone does all morning. It moves them over Manhattan instead of holding them out at sea.

115
00:07:34,891 --> 00:07:37,990
Put them where the problem is, and work out the rest later.

116
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:44,082
American eleven struck the north tower at that same minute. Floors ninety three to ninety nine.

117
00:07:44,082 --> 00:07:48,764
All three stairwells in the building were severed from the ninety second floor upward, and

118
00:07:48,764 --> 00:07:52,770
every person above that line was cut off from the ground in the first second.

119
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:59,637
Around ten minutes to nine, the air defence sector, still hunting for American eleven on radar,

120
00:07:59,637 --> 00:08:03,683
learns that a plane has hit the World Trade Center. They learn it by chance.

121
00:08:03,683 --> 00:08:07,090
They are still looking for the aircraft that has already hit it.

122
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:13,217
United one seventy five hits the south tower at three minutes and eleven seconds past nine.

123
00:08:13,217 --> 00:08:18,389
The air defence sector learns of that flight, in the Commission's words, at about the time the

124
00:08:18,389 --> 00:08:21,140
plane was hitting the south tower. Not before. At.

125
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:28,110
Which means the second aircraft reached the entire watching world, live, on television, before

126
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:32,675
it reached the agency responsible for defending the airspace it was flying through.

127
00:08:32,675 --> 00:08:35,645
That is not a metaphor for the communications failure.

128
00:08:35,645 --> 00:08:38,010
It is the communications failure, measured.

129
00:08:39,810 --> 00:08:44,935
In a classroom in Sarasota, Florida, at five minutes past nine, the President's chief of staff

130
00:08:44,935 --> 00:08:48,914
leans down and says seven words that are on the record exactly as spoken.

131
00:08:48,914 --> 00:08:52,240
A second plane hit the second tower. America is under attack.

132
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:59,830
The third aircraft was never reported hijacked to the military at all. Not once, by anyone.

133
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:06,205
American seventy seven pushes back from the gate at Dulles at nine minutes past eight and takes

134
00:09:06,205 --> 00:09:10,220
off at twenty past. Around ten to nine, somewhere over Ohio, it is taken.

135
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:15,390
At fifty six minutes past eight the transponder goes off, and then the primary radar return is

136
00:09:15,390 --> 00:09:20,120
lost as well. The aircraft simply stops existing on the screens that were watching it.

137
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:26,006
For thirty six minutes, nobody watching a radar screen in the United States knows where

138
00:09:26,006 --> 00:09:27,430
American seventy seven is.

139
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:34,235
At thirty four minutes past nine, the air defence sector finds out that American seventy seven

140
00:09:34,235 --> 00:09:34,838
is missing.

141
00:09:34,838 --> 00:09:39,719
They find out by chance, on a phone call they themselves had placed about something else.

142
00:09:39,719 --> 00:09:44,380
The Commission's finding on this is one flat sentence, and it is worth hearing twice.

143
00:09:45,510 --> 00:09:49,977
No one at the aviation agency's command centre or headquarters ever asked for military

144
00:09:49,977 --> 00:09:53,249
assistance with American seventy seven. Not before it was lost.

145
00:09:53,249 --> 00:09:55,950
Not during the thirty six minutes. Not at any point.

146
00:09:57,910 --> 00:10:00,803
Meanwhile fighters have gone up from Langley in Virginia.

147
00:10:00,803 --> 00:10:05,573
They were not sent because of American seventy seven, and they were not sent because of United

148
00:10:05,573 --> 00:10:06,233
ninety three.

149
00:10:06,233 --> 00:10:10,902
They were sent because of a report that American eleven was still in the air and heading for

150
00:10:10,902 --> 00:10:11,460
Washington.

151
00:10:12,610 --> 00:10:16,925
American eleven had hit the north tower thirty five minutes earlier. The report was wrong.

152
00:10:16,925 --> 00:10:19,562
The Commission could never establish who originated it.

153
00:10:19,562 --> 00:10:24,117
So the aircraft the military was actively hunting over Washington that morning was one that had

154
00:10:24,117 --> 00:10:28,720
already been destroyed, while the one actually approaching Washington was invisible to everyone.

155
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:35,301
It is worth slowing down on where that phantom came from, because it is the purest example of

156
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:36,190
the whole problem.

157
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:42,328
At twenty one minutes past nine, a military liaison at Boston Center tells the air defence

158
00:10:42,328 --> 00:10:46,763
sector that American eleven is still in the air, and heading towards Washington.

159
00:10:46,763 --> 00:10:48,038
The sector believes it.

160
00:10:48,038 --> 00:10:52,250
It is a plausible thing to be told by a credible person on a dedicated line.

161
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:58,609
Two minutes later the order goes out. Scramble Langley. Head them towards the Washington area.

162
00:10:58,609 --> 00:11:03,616
And a minute after that the sector's own log records the hijacked aircraft by tail number,

163
00:11:03,616 --> 00:11:05,730
November three three four Alpha Alpha.

164
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,047
That tail number belongs to American eleven.

165
00:11:09,047 --> 00:11:12,298
The aircraft that hit the north tower thirty five minutes earlier.

166
00:11:12,298 --> 00:11:16,977
The military is scrambling fighters toward Washington to intercept an aeroplane that is already

167
00:11:16,977 --> 00:11:19,390
lying in the wreckage of a building in Manhattan.

168
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:25,160
The Commission tried to find out who originated that report, and could not.

169
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:29,949
It is not attributed in the final text to any named person, and I am not going to attribute it

170
00:11:29,949 --> 00:11:34,533
either, because the draft material that names someone was never carried into the published

171
00:11:34,533 --> 00:11:34,890
report.

172
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:39,220
So here is where the morning actually stands at half past nine.

173
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:42,703
The fighters over New York are chasing a plane that no longer exists.

174
00:11:42,703 --> 00:11:46,338
The fighters heading for Washington were sent because of the same plane.

175
00:11:46,338 --> 00:11:51,083
And the aircraft genuinely approaching Washington is invisible, because nobody has told anyone

176
00:11:51,083 --> 00:11:51,840
to look for it.

177
00:11:53,980 --> 00:11:59,049
At thirty two minutes past nine, a radar facility at Dulles picks up a primary target tracking

178
00:11:59,049 --> 00:12:00,074
east at high speed.

179
00:12:00,074 --> 00:12:05,035
At thirty four minutes past, Reagan National warns the Secret Service of an unknown aircraft

180
00:12:05,035 --> 00:12:09,780
headed toward the White House. The aircraft is five miles from the Pentagon and turning.

181
00:12:10,910 --> 00:12:15,453
American seventy seven strikes the Pentagon at forty six seconds past nine thirty seven, at

182
00:12:15,453 --> 00:12:17,750
roughly five hundred and thirty miles an hour.

183
00:12:19,710 --> 00:12:24,093
One hundred and twenty five people in the building are killed, and fifty nine aboard the

184
00:12:24,093 --> 00:12:24,541
aircraft.

185
00:12:24,541 --> 00:12:29,123
The section it hit had a detail that matters, and it is the one genuinely fortunate thing in

186
00:12:29,123 --> 00:12:29,970
this entire film.

187
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:35,796
The aircraft hit the one wedge of the Pentagon that had just been rebuilt.

188
00:12:35,796 --> 00:12:40,953
The renovation was, in the engineers' own words, essentially complete at the time of the crash.

189
00:12:40,953 --> 00:12:45,946
It had put in blast resistant windows and reinforced the structure, and the report found the

190
00:12:45,946 --> 00:12:48,280
windows performed as they were designed to.

191
00:12:49,410 --> 00:12:53,366
Around fifty of the building's columns were destroyed or badly damaged.

192
00:12:53,366 --> 00:12:58,214
At fifty five minutes past nine, the incident commander outside ordered the impact area

193
00:12:58,214 --> 00:13:03,340
evacuated because he judged a collapse was coming. It came at fifty seven minutes past nine.

194
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:05,680
Not one first responder was injured in it.

195
00:13:07,480 --> 00:13:12,592
That is worth sitting with, because it is the counter example to everything else in this film.

196
00:13:12,592 --> 00:13:17,541
A man outside the Pentagon made a judgement, said it out loud, and the people who needed to

197
00:13:17,541 --> 00:13:22,000
hear it heard it, and moved. When the message arrives, this is what it looks like.

198
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:28,482
I want to stay at the Pentagon a little longer, because what happens outside that building for

199
00:13:28,482 --> 00:13:32,984
the rest of the morning is the single best documented emergency response of the day, and it is

200
00:13:32,984 --> 00:13:36,720
documented precisely because somebody decided afterwards to write it all down.

201
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:42,828
Command is established at the site at forty one minutes past nine, four minutes after impact,

202
00:13:42,828 --> 00:13:46,475
and it is established by a county fire department. Not the military.

203
00:13:46,475 --> 00:13:51,302
Arlington County, Virginia, takes incident command of an attack on the headquarters of the

204
00:13:51,302 --> 00:13:52,750
United States armed forces.

205
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:57,950
That sounds like an administrative detail. It is the reason it worked.

206
00:13:57,950 --> 00:14:03,183
There was one commander, in one place, and everybody arriving knew who it was and where to

207
00:14:03,183 --> 00:14:03,590
report.

208
00:14:05,410 --> 00:14:10,234
While all of this is happening in the air, the largest evacuation of a building in American

209
00:14:10,234 --> 00:14:12,090
history is happening underneath it.

210
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:17,703
The federal investigation put the number of people inside the two towers at the moment of the

211
00:14:17,703 --> 00:14:22,118
first impact at roughly seventeen thousand four hundred, with an uncertainty of about eleven

212
00:14:22,118 --> 00:14:23,030
hundred either way.

213
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:27,176
The overwhelming majority of them got out.

214
00:14:27,176 --> 00:14:32,703
That is the part of this day that is almost never told, and it is the reason the toll was not

215
00:14:32,703 --> 00:14:33,654
very much worse.

216
00:14:33,654 --> 00:14:39,360
But the ones who did not get out were, almost entirely, decided by geometry in the first second.

217
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:45,533
In the north tower, all three stairwells ran through the impact zone, and all three were

218
00:14:45,533 --> 00:14:50,069
destroyed there. Nobody above the ninety first floor evacuated. Not one person.

219
00:14:50,069 --> 00:14:51,160
There was no route.

220
00:14:53,140 --> 00:14:56,457
In the south tower, one stairwell survived. Stairwell A.

221
00:14:56,457 --> 00:15:01,966
It ran through the damaged floors and stayed passable, and at least eighteen people came down

222
00:15:01,966 --> 00:15:03,980
through the impact zone and lived.

223
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:09,393
Eighteen people, out of a tower where about six hundred and thirty died.

224
00:15:09,393 --> 00:15:14,060
The difference between those two numbers is one staircase that happened not to be in the way.

225
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:20,545
There is one more thing the investigators found, and it belongs in a film about messages.

226
00:15:20,545 --> 00:15:25,567
The nineteen ninety three bombing of the same complex had changed how people in those buildings

227
00:15:25,567 --> 00:15:27,945
were trained and how the stairwells were lit.

228
00:15:27,945 --> 00:15:32,967
Eight years of drills, and photoluminescent markings on the steps, were quietly doing work that

229
00:15:32,967 --> 00:15:33,390
morning.

230
00:15:34,540 --> 00:15:39,519
In February nineteen ninety three, a truck bomb went off in the parking garage under the north

231
00:15:39,519 --> 00:15:41,108
tower. Six people were killed.

232
00:15:41,108 --> 00:15:46,088
The towers filled with smoke, the lights in the stairwells failed, and it took people hours to

233
00:15:46,088 --> 00:15:47,200
get down in the dark.

234
00:15:48,340 --> 00:15:53,158
What happened afterwards is unglamorous and it saved a great many lives eight years later.

235
00:15:53,158 --> 00:15:57,441
The stairwells were marked with photoluminescent strips that glow without power.

236
00:15:57,441 --> 00:15:58,833
The drills became regular.

237
00:15:58,833 --> 00:16:03,490
People who worked in those buildings were taught the route down, repeatedly, for years.

238
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:09,946
On the eleventh of September, tens of thousands of people walked down those stairs, in smoke,

239
00:16:09,946 --> 00:16:14,510
and most of them had done it before. The investigators found that this mattered.

240
00:16:16,310 --> 00:16:20,821
It is the one preparation anybody made for that day that actually functioned as intended.

241
00:16:20,821 --> 00:16:22,545
And it worked for a simple reason.

242
00:16:22,545 --> 00:16:27,361
It was a message delivered years in advance, over and over, to the people who would need it, in

243
00:16:27,361 --> 00:16:30,250
a form they could act on without being told anything new.

244
00:16:32,210 --> 00:16:36,673
By nine o'clock there were already around two hundred and thirty five firefighters committed to

245
00:16:36,673 --> 00:16:37,753
the World Trade Center.

246
00:16:37,753 --> 00:16:42,028
Before the morning ended, more than a third of every fire company in New York City would be

247
00:16:42,028 --> 00:16:42,310
there.

248
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:48,339
At five minutes past nine, fire chiefs in the north tower lobby test the building's radio

249
00:16:48,339 --> 00:16:48,835
repeater.

250
00:16:48,835 --> 00:16:53,734
A repeater is the thing that takes a handheld radio signal and boosts it so it can travel

251
00:16:53,734 --> 00:16:56,047
through a hundred and ten floors of steel.

252
00:16:56,047 --> 00:16:59,790
Without one, a portable radio inside that tower is close to useless.

253
00:17:00,940 --> 00:17:03,838
The console had two buttons. One had been pressed.

254
00:17:03,838 --> 00:17:09,055
The second, the one that actually enabled transmission, had not been pressed that morning.

255
00:17:09,055 --> 00:17:14,445
The chief tries it, cannot transmit, and the lobby command abandons the repeater and moves to

256
00:17:14,445 --> 00:17:16,010
ordinary tactical channels.

257
00:17:17,810 --> 00:17:22,476
Now I have to be careful here, because this is the part of the day that is most often told

258
00:17:22,476 --> 00:17:25,120
wrongly, including by people with no agenda at all.

259
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:31,031
It is very easy to say the radios failed. The Commission's own investigators did not find that.

260
00:17:31,031 --> 00:17:35,720
After reviewing around seventy eight minutes of fire department radio tape, they believed the

261
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:40,461
repeater and the radios were in fact working, and that a chief who thought his call had failed

262
00:17:40,461 --> 00:17:44,495
had switched frequencies. The Port Authority maintained the system was in order.

263
00:17:44,495 --> 00:17:47,470
A chief who was there said his attempt did not get through.

264
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:54,265
Those accounts have never been reconciled, and I am not going to pretend to reconcile them.

265
00:17:54,265 --> 00:17:59,302
What the Commission concluded was not that a person failed. It was that the arrangement failed.

266
00:17:59,302 --> 00:18:04,286
A two button console, activated by a civilian operator, in a building where the equipment only

267
00:18:04,286 --> 00:18:06,990
half worked and nobody could be certain which half.

268
00:18:08,810 --> 00:18:13,925
At fifty nine minutes past nine, the south tower collapses. Everyone still inside it dies.

269
00:18:13,925 --> 00:18:18,130
And within about a minute, the order goes out to evacuate the north tower.

270
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:24,321
It goes out without the repetition protocol that the department used for its most urgent

271
00:18:24,321 --> 00:18:25,018
transmissions.

272
00:18:25,018 --> 00:18:29,450
One order, into a building full of men climbing stairs with sixty pounds of equipment, on

273
00:18:29,450 --> 00:18:31,740
radios that may or may not have been carrying.

274
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:38,671
And then at four minutes past ten, and again at eight minutes past, the police helicopters say

275
00:18:38,671 --> 00:18:43,092
out loud that the north tower is about to go. Nobody in that lobby can hear them.

276
00:18:43,092 --> 00:18:48,114
The fire department and the police department of the same city, at the same incident, had no

277
00:18:48,114 --> 00:18:49,260
shared radio channel.

278
00:18:51,240 --> 00:18:55,824
This one is not disputed by anybody. It is not a question of who pressed which button.

279
00:18:55,824 --> 00:19:00,727
Two organisations were looking at the same building, one of them knew, and there was no wire

280
00:19:00,727 --> 00:19:01,420
between them.

281
00:19:03,410 --> 00:19:06,351
The north tower falls at twenty eight minutes past ten.

282
00:19:06,351 --> 00:19:10,363
Everyone alive on the upper floors is killed, with a handful of exceptions.

283
00:19:10,363 --> 00:19:15,122
Twelve firefighters, one Port Authority police officer and three civilians survive inside

284
00:19:15,122 --> 00:19:17,850
stairwell B as the building comes down around them.

285
00:19:19,810 --> 00:19:24,950
I want to stop on those sixteen people, because it is the strangest fact in this entire day.

286
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:31,088
A hundred and ten storeys of building came down on top of them, and a section of one stairwell

287
00:19:31,088 --> 00:19:31,515
did not.

288
00:19:31,515 --> 00:19:36,310
Twelve firefighters, a Port Authority police officer, and three civilians were inside that

289
00:19:36,310 --> 00:19:39,400
section, and when it stopped moving they were alive in it.

290
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:45,973
Nobody engineered that. There is no lesson in it and no technique to take away from it.

291
00:19:45,973 --> 00:19:50,829
It is the one piece of pure luck in a day that otherwise ran entirely on preparation, or the

292
00:19:50,829 --> 00:19:51,410
lack of it.

293
00:19:53,210 --> 00:19:55,916
Three hundred and forty three firefighters are killed.

294
00:19:55,916 --> 00:20:00,326
Thirty seven Port Authority police officers. Twenty three New York City police officers.

295
00:20:00,326 --> 00:20:02,030
Eight emergency medical personnel.

296
00:20:04,010 --> 00:20:08,679
The fourth aircraft is the one where the whole argument of this film becomes impossible to

297
00:20:08,679 --> 00:20:08,990
avoid.

298
00:20:10,140 --> 00:20:13,144
United ninety three was supposed to leave Newark at eight.

299
00:20:13,144 --> 00:20:17,806
Routine congestion held it on the ground, and it took off at forty two minutes past eight,

300
00:20:17,806 --> 00:20:22,260
forty two minutes late. That delay is the reason everything that follows is different.

301
00:20:23,410 --> 00:20:27,056
It is hijacked at twenty eight minutes past nine over eastern Ohio.

302
00:20:27,056 --> 00:20:31,955
Four minutes later a hijacker makes an announcement to the cabin. We have a bomb on board.

303
00:20:31,955 --> 00:20:32,390
So, sit.

304
00:20:33,540 --> 00:20:37,396
And then something happens on this flight that happens on none of the others.

305
00:20:37,396 --> 00:20:41,302
Because it left late, the people on board have time, and they have telephones.

306
00:20:41,302 --> 00:20:43,605
Thirty seven calls are placed from that cabin.

307
00:20:43,605 --> 00:20:47,010
Thirty five from the seat back phones and two from personal mobiles.

308
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:53,504
Through those calls, the passengers and crew learn what has already happened in New York.

309
00:20:53,504 --> 00:20:58,259
They are the only people on any of the four aircraft who find out what the hijacking is

310
00:20:58,259 --> 00:21:02,740
actually for. And they work out, correctly, that the plan is not to land anywhere.

311
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:09,387
I am going to quote two of them once each, in the words the record actually holds, and then

312
00:21:09,387 --> 00:21:13,276
leave them alone. Everyone's running to first class. I've got to go. Bye.

313
00:21:13,276 --> 00:21:15,300
And, we're getting ready to do it now.

314
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:23,195
At fifty seven minutes past nine, in the Commission's own four words, the passenger assault

315
00:21:23,195 --> 00:21:23,574
began.

316
00:21:23,574 --> 00:21:29,456
The aircraft comes down in a field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania, at eleven seconds past ten

317
00:21:29,456 --> 00:21:33,630
oh three. Everyone aboard is killed. Nobody on the ground is hurt.

318
00:21:35,610 --> 00:21:39,970
Now watch the clock, because this is the sequence the whole film has been walking toward.

319
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:45,929
At seven minutes past ten, four minutes after United ninety three has already crashed, the air

320
00:21:45,929 --> 00:21:48,441
defence sector hears about it for the first time.

321
00:21:48,441 --> 00:21:53,311
At that same minute, a representative tells the air threat conference there is no indication of

322
00:21:53,311 --> 00:21:54,900
a hijack heading to Washington.

323
00:21:56,710 --> 00:22:01,876
Between ten past ten and eighteen minutes past, a military aide tells the Vice President that

324
00:22:01,876 --> 00:22:05,320
an inbound aircraft is eighty miles out, then sixty miles out.

325
00:22:05,320 --> 00:22:08,819
The Vice President authorises fighters to engage it, each time.

326
00:22:08,819 --> 00:22:13,707
The aircraft they are discussing is United ninety three, which has been on the ground in

327
00:22:13,707 --> 00:22:15,540
Pennsylvania for several minutes.

328
00:22:17,510 --> 00:22:23,383
At ten past ten, the fighters over Washington are told something quite specific. Negative.

329
00:22:23,383 --> 00:22:25,210
Negative clearance to shoot.

330
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:31,599
At thirty one minutes past ten, a general instructs his staff to put a message out over the

331
00:22:31,599 --> 00:22:33,213
military's own messaging system.

332
00:22:33,213 --> 00:22:37,903
The Vice President has cleared us to intercept tracks of interest and shoot them down if they

333
00:22:37,903 --> 00:22:38,660
do not respond.

334
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:43,451
That is the first moment the authorisation is documented reaching the operational chain at all.

335
00:22:43,451 --> 00:22:46,880
It is twenty eight minutes after United ninety three hit the ground.

336
00:22:48,840 --> 00:22:52,893
And then the Commission writes the sentence that this entire film is named after.

337
00:22:52,893 --> 00:22:57,546
While leaders in Washington believed that the fighters above them had been instructed to take

338
00:22:57,546 --> 00:23:02,300
out hostile aircraft, the only orders actually conveyed to the pilots were to identify type and

339
00:23:02,300 --> 00:23:02,550
tail.

340
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:06,407
And one page later, plainer still.

341
00:23:06,407 --> 00:23:11,129
The Vice President was mistaken in his belief that shootdown authorisation had been passed to

342
00:23:11,129 --> 00:23:13,110
the pilots flying at Norad's direction.

343
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:19,604
The men in the chain explained why, under oath, and their answers are not excuses.

344
00:23:19,604 --> 00:23:24,235
The sector commander said he did not pass the order along because he was unaware of its

345
00:23:24,235 --> 00:23:24,980
ramifications.

346
00:23:24,980 --> 00:23:29,983
The mission commander and the senior weapons director said they did not pass it to the Langley

347
00:23:29,983 --> 00:23:34,880
pilots because they were unsure how the pilots would, or should, proceed with that guidance.

348
00:23:36,840 --> 00:23:40,477
Read that again as a communications problem rather than a moral one.

349
00:23:40,477 --> 00:23:45,557
An instruction arrived that nobody had ever been trained to receive, in a form nobody could act

350
00:23:45,557 --> 00:23:50,050
on, about an aircraft that no longer existed. So it stopped. It did not get refused.

351
00:23:50,050 --> 00:23:51,280
It just stopped moving.

352
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:56,079
And there is a final wrinkle that is almost too neat.

353
00:23:56,079 --> 00:24:00,955
A separate group of fighters launched from Andrews at thirty eight minutes past ten, at the

354
00:24:00,955 --> 00:24:05,937
Secret Service's request, operating under weapons free, meaning the decision to fire sat with

355
00:24:05,937 --> 00:24:07,544
the lead pilot in the cockpit.

356
00:24:07,544 --> 00:24:11,830
Norad headquarters did not know those aircraft were flying, or under what rules.

357
00:24:13,810 --> 00:24:18,433
Two chains of command, over the same city, on the same morning, with opposite rules of

358
00:24:18,433 --> 00:24:20,960
engagement, and neither one aware of the other.

359
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:27,791
There is exactly one message that morning that went out to a continent and was obeyed almost

360
00:24:27,791 --> 00:24:31,060
instantly, and it came from a man on his first day in the job.

361
00:24:32,210 --> 00:24:37,144
At forty two minutes past nine, according to the Commission, the national operations manager at

362
00:24:37,144 --> 00:24:42,078
the aviation command centre ordered every facility in the country to instruct every aircraft to

363
00:24:42,078 --> 00:24:46,960
land at the nearest airport. His name was Ben Sliney. It was his first day running that floor.

364
00:24:48,110 --> 00:24:52,022
And here the record does something honest that most retellings tidy away.

365
00:24:52,022 --> 00:24:56,898
The Transportation Secretary testified under oath that at approximately nine forty five, he

366
00:24:56,898 --> 00:25:00,756
gave the aviation agency the final order for all civil aircraft to land.

367
00:25:00,756 --> 00:25:05,740
The agency's own official timeline, to this day, credits the order to him at nine forty five.

368
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:12,396
Two accounts, three minutes apart, both from primary sources, and the honest way to say it is

369
00:25:12,396 --> 00:25:12,693
this.

370
00:25:12,693 --> 00:25:18,328
One of them is the operational order given on the floor, and the other is the ratification from

371
00:25:18,328 --> 00:25:23,310
the bunker. Nobody lied. The record simply holds both, and we are not going to pick.

372
00:25:25,110 --> 00:25:28,070
More than four thousand aircraft were in American skies.

373
00:25:28,070 --> 00:25:33,040
Every one of them was told to get on the ground, by a system that had never rehearsed this and

374
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:34,732
had no written procedure for it.

375
00:25:34,732 --> 00:25:39,542
The written order formally suspending national airspace operations was not issued until six

376
00:25:39,542 --> 00:25:42,820
minutes past eleven, eighty four minutes after the verbal one.

377
00:25:44,610 --> 00:25:50,154
The last aircraft was down at sixteen minutes past twelve. Two hours and thirty four minutes.

378
00:25:50,154 --> 00:25:55,401
It is the single largest thing anyone accomplished that day, and it happened because one

379
00:25:55,401 --> 00:26:00,170
instruction, for once, went out cleanly and everybody who needed to hear it did.

380
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:06,619
There is one aircraft in that ground stop worth telling on its own, because it is the morning's

381
00:26:06,619 --> 00:26:08,740
whole thesis compressed into a single flight.

382
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:13,969
Korean Air eight five was over the Pacific, heading for Anchorage.

383
00:26:13,969 --> 00:26:19,483
Its crew sent a routine text message to their company that contained the letters H, J, K.

384
00:26:19,483 --> 00:26:23,200
In the shorthand of airline messaging, that can mean hijack.

385
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:29,477
Controllers asked the crew to confirm by squawking seven five zero zero, the hijack code.

386
00:26:29,477 --> 00:26:31,901
The crew did exactly what they were asked.

387
00:26:31,901 --> 00:26:36,981
And the moment they did it, the transponder was broadcasting that the aircraft was being

388
00:26:36,981 --> 00:26:37,500
hijacked.

389
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:41,539
Fighters were sent. Alaska moved people out of hotels.

390
00:26:41,539 --> 00:26:46,640
The aircraft was shadowed to Whitehorse in the Yukon, where part of the town was evacuated, and

391
00:26:46,640 --> 00:26:51,150
it landed at about ten to three in the afternoon with nothing wrong on board at all.

392
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:57,581
A crew complied perfectly with an instruction and the compliance itself read as the emergency.

393
00:26:57,581 --> 00:26:59,555
That is not a mistake anybody made.

394
00:26:59,555 --> 00:27:04,630
It is what happens when the signal and the confirmation of the signal are the same signal.

395
00:27:06,610 --> 00:27:08,906
The afternoon belongs to a third building.

396
00:27:08,906 --> 00:27:14,099
Seven World Trade Center was a forty seven storey tower across the street, and it is a separate

397
00:27:14,099 --> 00:27:15,903
building, not a third twin tower.

398
00:27:15,903 --> 00:27:21,096
When the north tower came down at twenty eight minutes past ten, debris tore into its south and

399
00:27:21,096 --> 00:27:23,610
west faces and severed seven exterior columns.

400
00:27:24,740 --> 00:27:27,605
Fires burned in it, unfought, for about seven hours.

401
00:27:27,605 --> 00:27:31,682
As early as half past eleven the fire department found there was no water.

402
00:27:31,682 --> 00:27:34,603
The collapse of the towers had severed the city main.

403
00:27:34,603 --> 00:27:39,782
You cannot fight a fire in a forty seven storey building with no water, so at around half past

404
00:27:39,782 --> 00:27:44,300
two the department gave the order to stop trying and pull back to a safe distance.

405
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:50,023
At fifty two seconds past five twenty in the afternoon, Seven World Trade Center came down.

406
00:27:50,023 --> 00:27:54,757
And the federal investigation records one sentence about it that deserves to be said out loud,

407
00:27:54,757 --> 00:27:58,433
because it is the fact that the noise around that building tends to bury.

408
00:27:58,433 --> 00:28:02,210
No lives were lost as a result of the collapse of Seven World Trade Center.

409
00:28:04,340 --> 00:28:09,835
The reason nobody died in it is that the fire department read the building correctly, said so,

410
00:28:09,835 --> 00:28:13,751
cleared the area, and were listened to. Three hours before it fell.

411
00:28:13,751 --> 00:28:19,304
On a day defined by messages that did not arrive, the last major event of the afternoon was one

412
00:28:19,304 --> 00:28:19,830
that did.

413
00:28:21,810 --> 00:28:26,964
The President's aircraft had left Sarasota at fifty four minutes past nine, in the Commission's

414
00:28:26,964 --> 00:28:29,025
phrase, without any fixed destination.

415
00:28:29,025 --> 00:28:34,071
It went to Barksdale in Louisiana, then to Offutt in Nebraska, and back to Washington late in

416
00:28:34,071 --> 00:28:36,675
the day, over a Pentagon that was still smoking.

417
00:28:36,675 --> 00:28:39,930
At half past eight that night he spoke from the Oval Office.

418
00:28:41,080 --> 00:28:42,354
The counting took years.

419
00:28:42,354 --> 00:28:47,027
In the days afterwards the figure was thought to be around three thousand seven hundred.

420
00:28:47,027 --> 00:28:49,947
A year later it was two thousand eight hundred and one.

421
00:28:49,947 --> 00:28:54,566
After investigators removed names that had been filed fraudulently, it settled near two

422
00:28:54,566 --> 00:28:56,319
thousand seven hundred and fifty.

423
00:28:56,319 --> 00:29:01,257
With the Pentagon and the four aircraft, the confirmed total is two thousand nine hundred and

424
00:29:01,257 --> 00:29:01,1000
seventy seven.

425
00:29:03,980 --> 00:29:09,136
As of last month, around sixty percent of the people who died in New York have been identified.

426
00:29:09,136 --> 00:29:12,176
The work of matching remains to names has never stopped.

427
00:29:12,176 --> 00:29:14,890
It is still going on now, twenty five years later.

428
00:29:16,880 --> 00:29:21,643
I want to be careful about what this film is arguing, because there is a version of this story

429
00:29:21,643 --> 00:29:23,720
that I am not telling and do not believe.

430
00:29:24,840 --> 00:29:25,849
Nobody stood down.

431
00:29:25,849 --> 00:29:31,117
The Commission found that the military improvised, badly and bravely, against information that

432
00:29:31,117 --> 00:29:33,303
was wrong almost every time it arrived.

433
00:29:33,303 --> 00:29:38,571
It found the public account that Norad gave afterwards was incorrect, and it did not find that

434
00:29:38,571 --> 00:29:41,597
the people on the operations floor did anything wrong.

435
00:29:41,597 --> 00:29:45,520
Those are two different findings and they are both in the same report.

436
00:29:47,510 --> 00:29:51,176
What I keep coming back to is how ordinary every one of these failures is.

437
00:29:51,176 --> 00:29:53,157
A microphone keyed to the wrong channel.

438
00:29:53,157 --> 00:29:56,080
A console with two buttons where only one had been pressed.

439
00:29:56,080 --> 00:29:59,151
Two radio systems in one city that could not reach each other.

440
00:29:59,151 --> 00:30:02,420
An order that arrived in a form nobody had been trained to act on.

441
00:30:04,380 --> 00:30:05,511
None of that is exotic.

442
00:30:05,511 --> 00:30:09,296
All of it is the kind of thing that is true in some building somewhere today.

443
00:30:09,296 --> 00:30:13,868
That is what makes the day worth going back through minute by minute, rather than remembering

444
00:30:13,868 --> 00:30:14,900
it as a single image.

445
00:30:16,710 --> 00:30:21,091
And the reason we can go back through it at all is that almost everything we know came off a

446
00:30:21,091 --> 00:30:25,614
recording. The tapes at the aviation agency and at the air defence sector were not volunteered.

447
00:30:25,614 --> 00:30:30,043
They were subpoenaed, in the autumn of two thousand three, and what was on them did not match

448
00:30:30,043 --> 00:30:32,090
what had been said in public for two years.

449
00:30:34,080 --> 00:30:38,200
And there is one recording that did not survive, which I am not going to leave out.

450
00:30:39,340 --> 00:30:44,408
At a New York air traffic facility that day, a manager recorded the accounts of controllers who

451
00:30:44,408 --> 00:30:48,569
had handled the hijacked aircraft, onto a single cassette, while it was fresh.

452
00:30:48,569 --> 00:30:51,770
It was an unusual thing to do and it was the right instinct.

453
00:30:52,910 --> 00:30:54,651
That tape was later destroyed.

454
00:30:54,651 --> 00:30:59,409
It was crushed in a hand, cut into pieces, and the pieces put into different bins.

455
00:30:59,409 --> 00:31:04,225
The Transportation Department's own inspector general investigated and reported it.

456
00:31:04,225 --> 00:31:09,680
Their account says five controllers had spoken onto it. Contemporary press reporting said six.

457
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:14,616
We do not know precisely what was on it, and we never will.

458
00:31:14,616 --> 00:31:19,308
On a day whose entire history we can only reconstruct because recordings existed, one of them

459
00:31:19,308 --> 00:31:22,032
was deliberately taken out of the record within weeks.

460
00:31:22,032 --> 00:31:26,320
I do not have a tidy conclusion about that. I just think you should know it happened.

461
00:31:28,440 --> 00:31:33,437
If the gap between what was said in public and what was on the tape is the part that stays with

462
00:31:33,437 --> 00:31:35,699
you, that is its own story, and we made it.

463
00:31:35,699 --> 00:31:39,960
Watch The Tapes That Broke Norad's nine eleven Story, right here on this channel.

464
00:31:39,960 --> 00:31:43,590
And subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, so you don't miss the next one.

